Shared orchard lifecycle (@farming-orchard-cycle: 0, #part-of @farming-orchards, #source @farm-source-orchard-planning, #source @farm-source-fruit-propagation) { r_28RP9QTKZ4G4RTNHKA2XZ6277C
1. Check frost, winter chill, heat, rainfall, dry-season water, drainage, mature size, rootstock, compatible pollen, and harvest access. Choose a species that fits the site.
2. Obtain traceable healthy plants of the chosen cultivar. Grafting preserves fruit traits and usually shortens juvenility; seedlings may vary. Check local movement restrictions and invasive status before bringing plants into a region.
3. Lay out full adult spacing and water access. Establish in a mild moist window, often the onset of reliable rain, while avoiding floods and severe cold. Plant at nursery depth, with the root flare and graft above soil; water the root ball and surrounding soil.
4. Mulch away from the trunk. Protect young plants against animals, sun scorch, wind, and drought. Train a reachable framework early and remove rootstock suckers. Fertilize from soil, leaf, and crop recommendations.
5. Observe flower initiation, pollination, fruit set, fruit filling, and ripening. Thin heavy crops where needed; sanitation and timely harvest reduce pest carryover.
6. Pick by species maturity signs; record bloom-to-harvest time and actual seasonal windows. Cool, cure, ripen, or process according to the fruit, not one universal storage temperature.
7. After harvest, remove dead or diseased material, prune at the appropriate stage, renew mulch and water plans, and assess yield and canopy health. Replace declining stock and maintain cultivar labels.

Practice: trial one suitable fruit species with compatible pollination before planting a full orchard. [Orchard planning](https://ask.ifas.ufl.edu/publication/HS1387); [propagation](https://ask.ifas.ufl.edu/publication/HS1349).
} r_28RP9QTKZ4G4RTNHKA2XZ6277C

