Use the shared orchard cycle. Fruit can be tropical while requiring a seasonal dry or cool signal for flowering. Local bloom, rain, and harvest observations are more useful than importing a source's month names.
} r_4DZ1XA8Z2C50XQJDNBZ8RR2QYC

Avocado — Persea americana (@persea-americana: 0, is #species, #part-of @farming-tropical-fruit, #source @farm-source-avocado, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-163, #related-food @nutrition-br-fdc-171705) { r_2G0TY4GJKRMQBRXQERJ3PWQ3VF
Plant an adapted grafted tree in exceptionally well-drained soil, usually with 5–8 m or more of canopy room unless managed smaller. Protect roots from standing water and mulch away from the trunk. Compatible A/B flowering cultivars can improve set, though a second tree is not universally essential. Grafted trees often begin within 3–4 years. Fruit matures on the tree but softens after picking: trial-pick at cultivar maturity and check normal ripening. Clip rather than drop fruit. Prune for access after harvest and retain healthy named replacement stock.
} r_2G0TY4GJKRMQBRXQERJ3PWQ3VF

Pineapple — Ananas comosus (@ananas-comosus: 0, is #species, #part-of @farming-tropical-fruit, #source @farm-source-pineapple, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-164, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-165) { r_40VNSM5JNXGFD4QB6732N9WS6Y
Plant healthy slips, suckers, or prepared crowns in warm drained soil, keeping the central heart above soil. Allow about 30–60 cm per plant with row access. Keep weeds down and prevent water sitting around the base. First fruit often takes 18–24 months from larger vegetative starts; crowns or poor conditions can take longer. Harvest at cultivar-appropriate maturity and colour development: pineapple does not become sweeter after picking. Each rosette fruits once; retain a healthy ratoon sucker for another crop or replant with selected material. Remove diseased plants and rotate the planting area when renewing.
} r_40VNSM5JNXGFD4QB6732N9WS6Y

Abiu — Pouteria caimito (@pouteria-caimito: 0, is #species, #part-of @farming-tropical-fruit, #source @farm-source-amazon-fruit, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-166) { r_3ST768MNT08RRRNEVT5DVYD896
Use fresh viable seed promptly or a named grafted plant. Establish in a humid frost-free site with drainage and roughly 6–8 m canopy room. Keep young plants watered and protected from severe wind. Train low branches and inspect fruit flies. First crops take several years and vary with propagation. Harvest fruit as the skin becomes yellow and eating maturity develops; immature fruit has abundant sticky latex. Handle gently and use promptly. Collect seed only from healthy mature fruit and renew by known material when uniform fruit quality matters.
} r_3ST768MNT08RRRNEVT5DVYD896

Acerola — Malpighia emarginata (@malpighia-emarginata: 0, is #species, #part-of @farming-tropical-fruit, #source @farm-source-tropical-fruit, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-169, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-170) { r_66DC077FQKDWGZ6V0EFZG5KBDV
Use an adapted rooted cutting or grafted cultivar rather than variable seed if uniform fruit matters. Plant in a warm drained site about 3–5 m apart; maintain water during establishment and fruiting. Preserve pollinating insects and prune to keep fruit reachable. Clonal plants may bear in roughly 1–2 years. Pick fully coloured sound fruit frequently, then cool or pulp promptly: ripe acerola is fragile. Several flowering flushes can produce several harvests in warm sites. After harvest, remove damaged branches and maintain healthy replacement stock.
} r_66DC077FQKDWGZ6V0EFZG5KBDV

Atemoya — Annona squamosa × Annona cherimola (@annona-atemoya: 0, is #hybrid, #part-of @farming-tropical-fruit, #source @farm-source-atemoya, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-174) { r_7TQ35PXWT0MVHFSVQC81AK7SPR
Plant a named grafted hybrid in a warm subtropical site, usually 4–6 m apart. Keep drainage good and irrigate during flowering and fruit growth. Train an accessible canopy. Flowers change sexual phase; hand pollination using properly collected pollen can improve set. First crops often occur within 2–4 years. Clip mature fruit as segments expand and skin lightens, before excessive softening; ripen gently after harvest. Remove mummified fruit and prune at the locally recommended stage. Seeds do not reliably preserve the selected hybrid.
} r_7TQ35PXWT0MVHFSVQC81AK7SPR

Banana — Musa acuminata (@musa-acuminata: 0, is #species, #part-of @farming-tropical-fruit, #source @farm-source-banana) { r_4K8VDP2X2SGP2MSMGJXTX8T51Z
An ancestor of cultivated bananas; many dessert cultivars carry only A genomes, but a food-table banana name does not establish genome group. Plant certified clean tissue-culture plants or healthy sword suckers in warm drained soil, often 2–3 m apart. Feed and irrigate regularly, protect against wind, and manage a mother, follower, and younger replacement rather than a crowded mat. First bunches commonly take 9–18 months. Cut mature full fingers while green for transport or closer to ripeness for immediate use. Each pseudostem fruits once: remove it after harvest while retaining healthy followers.
} r_4K8VDP2X2SGP2MSMGJXTX8T51Z

Hybrid bananas and plantains — Musa acuminata × Musa balbisiana (@musa-acuminata-balbisiana: 0, is #hybrid, #part-of @farming-tropical-fruit, #source @farm-source-banana) { r_79K4D982X7Q34CC75YDXF1DAQG
Includes many AAB and ABB culinary bananas; not every banana-da-terra, prata, maçã, figo, or pacova food entry can be assigned a genome from its name alone. Follow @musa-acuminata for establishment and mat renewal, adjusting spacing, support, and water to the named cultivar. Choose harvest maturity for cooking, ripening, or storage; many plantains are deliberately harvested mature green. Keep cultivar and nursery identity through vegetative propagation. Never multiply apparently vigorous suckers from a plantation with suspected systemic disease.
} r_79K4D982X7Q34CC75YDXF1DAQG

Cacao — Theobroma cacao (@theobroma-cacao: 0, is #species, #part-of @farming-tropical-fruit, #source @farm-source-tropical-fruit, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-183) { r_07JNW576Z782DKBJ7G6R1MQC5B
Plant healthy seedlings or compatible selected clones in humid frost-free conditions, often in a planned agroforestry shade system. Typical orchard spacing starts around 3 m, modified by canopy and shade design. Maintain moisture without flooding, remove diseased pods, and preserve tiny pollinating insects. First pods commonly appear in 3–5 years. Cut ripe pods by cultivar colour change without injuring the trunk's flower cushions. Open sound pods hygienically; beans need controlled fermentation and thorough drying before storage and roasting. Pulp and fermented beans are different products. Renew selected stock and manage shade rather than clearing the surrounding system.
} r_07JNW576Z782DKBJ7G6R1MQC5B

Cajá-manga — Spondias dulcis (@spondias-dulcis: 0, is #species, #part-of @farming-tropical-fruit, #source @farm-source-spondias, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-184) { r_0AHKVFTB1V3GH4DM4DQHZCHXHN
Use a known grafted or vegetatively propagated cultivar when possible. Establish in a warm drained site with 6–10 m room for ordinary trees; dwarf cultivars differ. Water young trees, then manage canopy for picking. Crops begin after several years, earlier with precocious clonal stock. Pick full-sized fruit at the intended green or yellow eating stage; let mature fruit finish ripening gently. Prevent fallen-fruit pest buildup. Prune after harvest and keep healthy clonal replacements; the large fibrous stone does not guarantee a seedling identical to its parent.
} r_0AHKVFTB1V3GH4DM4DQHZCHXHN

