Mandarin — Citrus reticulata (@citrus-reticulata: 0, is #species, #part-of @farming-tropical-fruit, #source @farm-source-tropical-fruit, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-238, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-251, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-252) { r_0W856E22FC88X1AE9K5TB30QGH
Includes many tangerina and mexerica cultivars; Murcott is commonly a tangor hybrid rather than a pure mandarin. Use labelled certified grafted plants and the shared citrus establishment cycle. Cultivar and pollination arrangement affect seediness and yield. Thin excessive crops where recommended to reduce branch breakage and alternate bearing. Harvest when juice, sugar-acid balance, and rind separation suit the cultivar; clip delicate fruit to avoid tearing peel. Initial crops usually take a few years. Keep cultivar labels through renewal and do not infer identical nutrition from similar market names.
} r_0W856E22FC88X1AE9K5TB30QGH

Tahiti lime — Citrus × latifolia (@citrus-latifolia: 0, is #cultivated-hybrid, #part-of @farming-tropical-fruit, #source @farm-source-tropical-fruit, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-220) { r_0DKPRY16FT7F7C2732GRWVY8J6
Plant certified grafted stock in a warm drained site with locally suitable rootstock and about 4–6 m canopy room. Follow the citrus cycle for water and health. Initial production can begin within a few years. Harvest full-sized juicy fruit at the intended green-market stage; green colour alone does not prove sufficient juice. Cut or twist gently without tearing the rind and cool above chilling temperatures recommended for limes. Named grafted stock preserves the largely seedless commercial type. Prune for access and replace seriously diseased plants.
