Key lime — Citrus × aurantiifolia (@citrus-aurantiifolia: 0, is #cultivated-hybrid, #part-of @farming-tropical-fruit, #source @farm-source-tropical-fruit, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-219) { r_5R8QE1SV3778D30CBMWAT3ZNVH
Commonly limão-galego. More cold-sensitive than several other citrus types; choose a frost-protected warm site. Use healthy labelled nursery stock, allow mature canopy room, and follow the shared citrus water and disease cycle. Pick mature juicy fruit green-to-yellow according to use; flavour and juice matter more than a fixed month. Handle thin skins gently and use or cool promptly. Seedlings can vary and may have a longer juvenile period than selected propagated plants. Renew with clean identified stock and keep rootstock suckers separate from the fruiting canopy.
} r_5R8QE1SV3778D30CBMWAT3ZNVH

Rangpur lime — Citrus × limonia (@citrus-limonia: 0, is #cultivated-hybrid, #part-of @farming-tropical-fruit, #source @farm-source-tropical-fruit, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-218) { r_31Q0N49199A2AW1TKR8N6BVVE5
Often limão-cravo; a cultivated mandarin/citron-derived citrus, not a true lemon. Establish healthy named material in a suitable drained site and follow the shared citrus cycle. Rootstock use does not automatically make every planting appropriate for fruit production or local disease pressure. Harvest full-sized juicy fruit at cultivar maturity, commonly with orange rind, according to the desired juice use. Remove fallen damaged fruit and prune conservatively after harvest. Retain clean grafted or otherwise correctly identified replacement stock; food identity should follow the fruiting cultivar, not just the rootstock label.
