Choose a grafted cultivar/rootstock combination with suitable chill, adult size, and a compatible flowering partner. Spacing depends strongly on rootstock and training; dwarf trees need permanent support. Irrigate establishment and filling, thin excess fruit early, and monitor local diseases and fruit pests. Initial harvest commonly takes 2–5 years, longer on vigorous stock. Pick by cultivar colour, starch or seed maturity, firmness, and flavour; a red surface alone is insufficient. Cool sound fruit. Prune for light and renew fruiting wood; seed from a Fuji apple does not produce a Fuji tree.
} r_5N07M14KKVQX7YN4ZBVV0A7MSY

Strawberry — Fragaria × ananassa (@fragaria-ananassa: 0, is #hybrid, #part-of @farming-temperate-fruit, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-239, #related-food @nutrition-br-fdc-167762, #source @farm-source-strawberry, #source @farm-source-tropical-fruit) { r_2PV76DMA9PNTG3FRYKWV51M70R
Plant certified healthy runners or plugs of a cultivar suited to chill and day length; seasonal and day-neutral types behave differently. Set crowns at soil level, around 25–35 cm apart, in draining beds with clean mulch. Keep water steady and fruit off wet soil; monitor mites and rots. Some systems crop within a few months of planting, while others carry through a winter. Pick fully coloured fruit with a calyx, frequently and gently; cool immediately. Manage runners according to the chosen annual or perennial system, and renew from clean stock rather than virus-affected old beds.
} r_2PV76DMA9PNTG3FRYKWV51M70R

Loquat — Eriobotrya japonica (@eriobotrya-japonica: 0, is #species, #part-of @farming-temperate-fruit, #source @farm-source-loquat, #related-food @nutrition-br-taco-240) { r_7AMCKE0H6KRKAJZ9X04YCVSDCP
Plant a named grafted cultivar in a mild drained site with roughly 4–6 m canopy room. Winter flowering makes fruit vulnerable to frost even where the tree survives. Irrigate during fruit filling, thin clusters for usable size, and manage fruit flies. Grafted plants often bear within 3–4 years. Pick full-coloured ripe fruit by flavour, clipping clusters gently; it does not develop good sweetness from an immature harvest. Cool or process promptly and exclude seeds from pulp. Prune after harvest and retain named replacement stock.
